{"version": 2, "width": 110, "height": 24, "timestamp": 1794533304, "title": "data/heat-pump \u00b7 not-ambitious run 1", "env": {"TERM": "xterm-256color"}, "theme": {"fg": "#d8dee9", "bg": "#1e1f1c", "palette": "#272822:#f92672:#a6e22e:#f4bf75:#66d9ef:#ae81ff:#a1efe4:#f8f8f2:#75715e:#f92672:#a6e22e:#f4bf75:#66d9ef:#ae81ff:#a1efe4:#f9f8f5"}}
[0.0, "o", "\u001b[?25l"]
[0.0, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ Work out whether switching from my gas boiler to a heat pump would save me money, as a Markdown file\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[24.978, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mWrote 1 file\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Write(<workspace>/heat-pump-vs-gas-boiler.md)\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[29.343, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I've written the analysis to `<workspace>/heat-pump-vs-gas-boiler.md`. It can't give you a firm yes or no\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  yet, because I don't have your bills, location or home details. All the prices in it are illustrative\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  assumptions, not quotes or real tariffs.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **The test:** a heat pump is cheaper to run when your electricity price divided by your gas price is below\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the heat pump's seasonal efficiency (SCOP) multiplied by your boiler's efficiency. For example, SCOP 3.2 ×\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  0.85 gives 2.7.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Worked example:** with gas at 0.07 per kWh, a standard electricity price of 0.25 per kWh gives roughly\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  break-even at SCOP 3.0 and modest savings at SCOP 3.5–4.0. At 0.15 per kWh the savings are larger, around\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  300–450 a year. These figures come from an assumed 12,000 kWh of gas use a year and an 85% efficient boiler.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Payback:** installation costs and grants usually matter more than running costs. In my example, a net\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  cost of 6,000, avoided boiler cost of 2,500 and savings of 300 a year give a payback of about 12 years.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Verdict:** a heat pump probably saves money if you can get cheap electricity (a heat pump or time-of-use\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  tariff, or solar) and your boiler is due for replacement anyway. On a standard tariff with a working boiler,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  it often doesn't pay back on running costs alone.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The file also has a section where you fill in your own numbers. If you send me your gas and electricity\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  bills, your region and a rough description of the house, I'll fill it in for your situation.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[36.854, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ I'm in the UK, in a 3-bed 1990s semi-detached house of about 95 m² with cavity wall insulation and a 100 mm\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  loft. Last year I used about 11,500 kWh of gas and 2,900 kWh of electricity. I pay roughly 6p per kWh for\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  gas and 26p per kWh for electricity on a standard variable tariff, plus a standing charge on each. My boiler\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  is a 12-year-old combi that's still working fine. I don't have solar, but I'd consider a heat pump tariff if\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  it makes the sums work. Please fill in your section with these numbers, include the grant I'd get here (the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  £7,500 Boiler Upgrade Scheme, if it's still available), and give me a clear yes or no.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[39.471, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mLoaded 1 tool\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  ToolSearch({\"query\": \"select:WebSearch\", \"max_results\": 1})\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[41.155, "o", "\u001b[1A\u001b[2K\u001b[1A\u001b[2K\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mLoaded 1 tool, ran 1 web search\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  WebSearch(Boiler Upgrade Scheme grant £7,500 air source heat pump current status November 2026)\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[41.406, "o", "\u001b[1A\u001b[2K\u001b[1A\u001b[2K\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mLoaded 1 tool, ran 2 web searchs\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  WebSearch(heat pump tariff unit rate p/kWh Octopus Cosy British Gas heat pump tariff 2026)\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[94.632, "o", "\u001b[1A\u001b[2K\u001b[1A\u001b[2K\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mLoaded 1 tool, ran 2 web searchs, ran 1 shell command\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Bash(cd \"<workspace>\" && n=$(grep -n '^## 5. Your numbers' heat-pump-vs-gas-boiler.md | cut -d: -f1) …\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[103.237, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ **Yes, but only if you move to a heat pump tariff. On your current 26p tariff the answer is no.** I've\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  filled in sections 5 and 6 of `<workspace>/heat-pump-vs-gas-boiler.md`.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Some of the inputs below are my own estimates, not figures from your bills or from quotes:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Boiler efficiency:** I assumed 82% for a 12-year-old combi.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Installation cost:** I assumed about £12,500 installed, plus about £1,500 for radiator upgrades.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Heat pump efficiency (SCOP):** I used 3.0 to 3.6. SCOP is how many units of heat you get per unit of\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  electricity, averaged over a year.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Heat pump tariff:** I used 14p/kWh as a placeholder. The tariff sites I found disagreed, from 7p to 17p,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and they were mostly low-quality comparison sites. Check the live rate with the supplier.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Standing charges:** I assumed about £110 a year for gas, and about £37 a year more for electricity on a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  heat pump tariff.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Your gas costs £690 a year and your electricity £754, so you pay £1,444 today.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Standard tariff at 26p:** the heat pump costs about £50 to £130 a year more than gas at SCOP 3.0 to 3.3.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  At SCOP 3.6 it is roughly break-even. Your electricity costs 4.3 times as much as gas per kWh, and the heat\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  pump needs that ratio to be below about 2.5 to 3.0 to win.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Heat pump tariff at about 14p:** you'd save about £560 to £640 a year. A heat pump tariff applies to\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  your other 2,900 kWh of household electricity too, and that is where about half the saving comes from.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Heat pump tariff at 17p:** you'd save about £380 to £470 a year.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Payback:** with the £7,500 grant, the net cost is about £6,500. That pays back in about 9 to 12 years at\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  14p, or about 15 years at 17p. The 9-year case assumes you also disconnect gas, which saves about £110 a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  year. I haven't counted an avoided boiler replacement, which would shorten these by several years.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Your boiler works fine, so this is a good-value move rather than an urgent one. The strongest moment is when\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the boiler fails, if the grant is still available then.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **The grant.** The £7,500 Boiler Upgrade Scheme was still current in the sources I found, which date from\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  mid-2026. I couldn't confirm anything newer, so check [Ofgem](https://www.ofgem.gov.uk/environmental-and-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  social-schemes/boiler-upgrade-scheme) and [gov.uk](https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/boiler-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  upgrade-scheme-regulations-approved-standards-grant-categories-and-grant-levels/notice-of-approved-grant-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  categories-and-values-for-the-boiler-upgrade-scheme--2). The £9,000 uplift is for off-gas homes, so it\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  doesn't apply to you.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Before committing:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Quotes:** get 2 to 3 MCS-certified quotes with a room-by-room heat loss survey and a stated SCOP. Aim\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  for a net cost under about £7,000 and a SCOP of 3.2 or better.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Loft:** top it up to 270 mm first. It costs a few hundred pounds and makes the heat pump's job easier.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Tariff sources: [Energy Plus](https://www.energyplus.co.uk/costsavingadvice/heat-pump-electricity-tariff-uk-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  cheapest-rates) and [Octopus Cosy performance page](https://octopus.energy/cosy-heat-pump-performance).\u001b[0m\r\n"]
