[158.986, "o", "\u001b[1A\u001b[2K\u001b[1A\u001b[2K\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mRead 1 file, ran 1 shell command, edited 3 files\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Edit(<workspace>/heat-pump-vs-gas-boiler.md)\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[167.11, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I stress-tested the numbers, and they change my answer. I've updated `<workspace>/heat-pump-vs-gas-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  boiler.md` with a new section 7 and a revised verdict in section 6.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Revised answer: not now on money alone. Yes when the boiler needs replacing, or now only if you can lock\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  in a heat pump tariff of about 14p or less.** My first \"yes\" relied on simple payback, which flatters the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  case. The new model uses a 15-year heat pump life, discounts savings at 3.5% a year in real terms, and adds\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  £50 a year for servicing. It also assumes the gas standing charge stays (it's only saved if you disconnect\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  gas).\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Where the tariff stops saving money:** the heat pump costs less to run than your current setup only\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  below about 23.6p (SCOP 3.3).\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Where the £6,500 pays back over 15 years:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - **Ignoring the boiler:** you need about 13.8p.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - **Counting a £2,500 boiler replacement avoided in year 5:** you need about 16.9p.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **15-year net present value at 14p and SCOP 3.3:** about −£155 without the boiler credit, and about\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  +£1,950 with it.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **At 17p:** about −£2,100 without the boiler credit, and roughly zero with it.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Biggest sensitivities:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - **Installation price:** every £1,500 off the net cost adds £1,500 to the 15-year value.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - **Tariff:** each 1p shifts the annual saving by about £65 to £75.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - **Gas vs electricity prices:** if gas rises 1% a year faster than electricity, the 14p case goes from\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  −£155 to +£334. The reverse would hurt it.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Three smaller findings:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Loft top-up:** I previously suggested doing this first. It only saves about £20 to £35 a year, so it's a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  comfort measure, not a payback one. I've changed the text.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Household electricity saving:** I credited you with the whole drop from 26p to 14p on your other 2,900\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  kWh. Against a fair alternative, staying on gas and fixing at about 24p, the saving is about £58 a year\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  lower.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Carbon:** the switch saves roughly 1.7 to 1.8 tonnes of CO₂ a year. If that matters to you, it's worth\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  more than the financial margin here.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I'd change my mind if a quote nets below about £5,500 after the grant, you can lock in 12p or less, your\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  boiler fails, or a survey supports a SCOP of 3.6.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The tariff figures are still the weakest input, because my search results for heat pump tariffs ranged from\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  7p to 17p. A confirmed live rate from a supplier would move the verdict more than any other single fact.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
